2:T92b,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the most common mobile conversion leak on landing pages?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The primary CTA not being visible on mobile without scrolling. The audit checks for this specifically: on a 375px viewport, is the primary call to action visible before the first scroll? If not, a significant portion of mobile visitors never see the action they are supposed to take."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the minimum tap target size for mobile buttons?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"44×44 CSS pixels - Google's Web Vitals standard and Apple's HIG minimum. Buttons, links, and form controls below this size cause accidental taps on adjacent elements or failed interactions. Variant selectors, size pickers, and icon-only buttons on ecommerce pages are the most frequent offenders."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does page load speed affect mobile conversion differently than desktop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Mobile connections are slower and more variable than desktop. A page that loads in 1.2s on desktop may take 3.8s on a median 4G connection. Google's LCP threshold is 2.5s. Pages above that threshold see measurable conversion drop-off because mobile visitors are more likely to abandon on slow connections than desktop users."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I check if my landing page has mobile conversion problems without testing on a device?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Chrome DevTools device simulation covers most observable issues: CTA visibility at 375px, tap target size, viewport rendering, and image layout. For real device rendering, the audit fetches the page HTML and checks viewport meta configuration, image sizing signals, and font-size declarations in the source."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is horizontal overflow and why does it hurt mobile conversion?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Horizontal overflow occurs when an element's width exceeds the viewport width, creating a horizontal scrollbar. It signals to the visitor that the page was not designed for their device. It also pushes CTAs out of the visible horizontal area on some layouts, making them unreachable without deliberate scrolling in two directions."}}]}6:X
