tors are more likely to abandon on slow connections than desktop users."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"How do I check if my landing page has mobile conversion problems without testing on a device?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"Chrome DevTools device simulation covers most observable issues: CTA visibility at 375px, tap target size, viewport rendering, and image layout. For real device rendering, the audit fetches the page HTML and checks viewport meta configuration, image sizing signals, and font-size declarations in the source."}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-border bg-bg-muted/20 p-5","children":[["$","h3",null,{"className":"text-sm font-semibold text-fg mb-2","children":"What is horizontal overflow and why does it hurt mobile conversion?"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted leading-6","children":"Horizontal overflow occurs when an element's width exceeds the viewport width, creating a horizontal scrollbar. It signals to the visitor that the page was not designed for their device. It also pushes CTAs out of the visible horizontal area on some layouts, making them unreachable without deliberate scrolling in two directions."}]]}]]}]]}]
